::CW_U05_L02_Q01::Draft sentence: Riley told Morgan that she should apologize because she was wrong. What is unclear?{
~A. The weather.#Incorrect. Weather is not mentioned.
=
~C. The spelling of apologize.#Incorrect. The word is spelled correctly.
~D. The setting only.#Incorrect. The main issue is pronoun reference.
}

::CW_U05_L02_Q02::Draft: The rain stopped. I found the key. The porch was empty. Which revision improves cohesion?{
~B. The rain stopped. I found the key. The porch was empty.#Incorrect. This repeats the choppy original.
~C. Key rain porch stopped found.#Incorrect. This is not clear.
~D. The porch was a key and rain.#Incorrect. This changes meaning.
=
}

::CW_U05_L02_Q03::Draft: i waited by the window it was dark Which revision best fixes conventions?{
=
~A. i waited by the window it was dark.#Incorrect. Capitalization and sentence boundary errors remain.
~C. I waited by the window it was dark.#Incorrect. This still needs clearer punctuation.
~D. waited I dark window by.#Incorrect. Word order is unclear.
}

::CW_U05_L02_Q04::Which revision improves clarity? Original: They put it there after it happened.{
~B. They did it there.#Incorrect. This remains vague.
~C. It was a thing.#Incorrect. This is unclear.
=
~D. Something happened somewhere.#Incorrect. This is too vague.
}

::CW_U05_L02_Q05::Which sentence best explains cohesion?{
=
~B. Cohesion means spelling every word aloud.#Incorrect. That is not cohesion.
~C. Cohesion means deleting every transition.#Incorrect. Transitions often support cohesion.
~D. Cohesion means writing without paragraphs forever.#Incorrect. Paragraphs can help organization.
}

::CW_U05_L02_Q06::Draft: I opened the envelope there was nothing inside. What convention issue should be fixed?{
~B. Incorrect capitalization of I.#Incorrect. I is capitalized.
~C. Misspelled envelope.#Incorrect. Envelope is spelled correctly.
~D. Missing character name only.#Incorrect. The main convention issue is sentence boundary.
=
}

::CW_U05_L02_Q07::Which revision keeps creative voice while improving conventions? Original: no sound. just the clock.{
~B. There was no auditory experience except a timekeeping device.#Incorrect. This flattens the creative voice.
=
~C. no sound just clock no punctuation.#Incorrect. This remains uncontrolled.
~D. Delete the line.#Incorrect. The line can be revised.
}

::CW_U05_L02_Q08::Draft: The scene jumps from the character leaving school to arriving home with no signal. What should the writer add?{
~B. A random new character.#Incorrect. That does not connect the jump.
~C. More unclear pronouns.#Incorrect. That creates confusion.
=
~D. A spelling error.#Incorrect. That weakens conventions.
}

::CW_U05_L02_Q09::Which problem is a clarity issue?{
~B. A word is spelled correctly.#Incorrect. Correct spelling is not a problem.
~C. The draft has a useful transition.#Incorrect. That supports cohesion.
~D. The paragraph is easy to follow.#Incorrect. That is not a problem.
=
}

::CW_U05_L02_Q10::Which problem is a cohesion issue?{
=
~B. One comma is missing in a list.#Incorrect. That is a convention issue.
~C. The narrator has a clear goal.#Incorrect. That supports clarity.
~D. A pronoun clearly refers to Mara.#Incorrect. That is not a problem.
}

::CW_U05_L02_Q11::Which problem is a conventions issue?{
~B. A scene needs more motivation.#Incorrect. That is meaning/development.
~C. The ending comes too suddenly.#Incorrect. That is structure/cohesion.
=
~D. The image is too vague.#Incorrect. That is clarity or diction.
}

::CW_U05_L02_Q12::Draft: The lamp flickered. The room was cold. I heard footsteps. Which revision improves cohesion without changing meaning?{
~B. The lamp was a sandwich.#Incorrect. This changes meaning.
=
~C. I heard footsteps and deleted the lamp.#Incorrect. It removes a detail.
~D. Cold cold cold lamp.#Incorrect. This is unclear.
}

::CW_U05_L02_Q13::Why should convention edits usually come after larger revision?{
=
~B. Because conventions never matter.#Incorrect. They do matter.
~C. Because spelling is impossible to fix.#Incorrect. Spelling can be fixed.
~D. Because readers prefer errors.#Incorrect. Readers need control.
}

::CW_U05_L02_Q14::Which sentence is clearest?{
~B. After she missed it, she called her for it.#Incorrect. Pronouns are unclear.
~C. It happened and she did that.#Incorrect. This is vague.
~D. The ride and bus and aunt.#Incorrect. This is a fragment without clear action.
=
}

::CW_U05_L02_Q15::Which transition best connects a flashback to the present?{
~B. Blue and green and loud.#Incorrect. This does not signal time.
~C. Because punctuation is useful.#Incorrect. This does not create a flashback transition.
=
~D. Very suddenly no reason.#Incorrect. This is unclear.
}

::CW_U05_L02_Q16::Draft: The character's name changes from Nia to Nina by mistake. What kind of issue is this?{
~B. Intentional symbolism automatically.#Incorrect. It is a mistake unless clearly purposeful.
=
~C. A transition only.#Incorrect. It is not mainly a transition.
~D. A feedback source.#Incorrect. It is a draft problem.
}

::CW_U05_L02_Q17::Which revision improves a run-on sentence? Original: I opened the box it was empty.{
~B. I opened the box it was empty.#Incorrect. This repeats the run-on.
~C. opened box empty was.#Incorrect. This is not clear.
~D. I opened.#Incorrect. This removes important meaning.
=
}

::CW_U05_L02_Q18::Which sentence best explains an intentional fragment?{
=
~B. I used a fragment because mistakes do not matter.#Incorrect. That is not a craft reason.
~C. I did not notice the fragment.#Incorrect. That is accidental, not intentional.
~D. I used fragments to confuse readers.#Incorrect. Confusion is not the goal.
}

::CW_U05_L02_Q19::Draft: The paragraph begins at night, then suddenly says that morning sunlight filled the room. What would improve cohesion?{
~B. Remove all time details forever.#Incorrect. That may create more confusion.
=
~C. Add a spelling error.#Incorrect. That does not improve cohesion.
~D. Change every name.#Incorrect. That does not connect time.
}

::CW_U05_L02_Q20::Which revision improves clarity in dialogue? Original: "Give it to her," he said.{
~B. "Give it to her," he said.#Incorrect. This keeps the unclear pronouns.
~C. "Thing person," he said.#Incorrect. This is not clear.
=
~D. "Give," said it.#Incorrect. This is unclear.
}

::CW_U05_L02_Q21::Which choice best describes publication-ready writing?{
=
~B. Writing with no paragraphs and many accidental errors.#Incorrect. That is not reader-ready.
~C. Writing that ignores audience.#Incorrect. Publication requires audience awareness.
~D. Writing that cannot be understood.#Incorrect. Readers need access.
}

::CW_U05_L02_Q22::Which convention issue most affects readability?{
~B. A purposeful line break in a poem.#Incorrect. Purposeful line breaks can support meaning.
~C. A clear transition.#Incorrect. That helps readability.
=
~D. A correctly spelled word.#Incorrect. Correct spelling is not an issue.
}

::CW_U05_L02_Q23::Which revision best connects two ideas? Original: I found the photograph. I remembered the house.{
~B. Photograph house remembered found I.#Incorrect. This is not clear.
~C. I found the photograph. I remembered the house.#Incorrect. This is acceptable but less cohesive than the revision.
~D. I deleted both ideas.#Incorrect. That removes meaning.
=
}

::CW_U05_L02_Q24::Which answer best distinguishes ambiguity from confusion?{
~B. Ambiguity and confusion are always the same.#Incorrect. They are different.
=
~C. Confusion is always better than clarity.#Incorrect. Readers need clarity.
~D. Ambiguity means no evidence matters.#Incorrect. Evidence still matters.
}

::CW_U05_L02_Q25::Which revision explanation is strongest?{
=
~A. I fixed stuff.#Incorrect. This is vague.
~C. It is better now.#Incorrect. This does not explain why.
~D. I changed words because I had to.#Incorrect. This gives no craft reason.
}
